  "how it works",
  "shorts"
 ],
 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Look at an old steel bridge. It's made of triangles. Dozens of them.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Cranes, roof frames, power line towers. Triangles everywhere. Why not squares?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Take four bars and bolt them into a square. Now push on one corner.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It folds over, like a box being flattened. The bars didn't bend at all. The corners just turned.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now take three bars and make a triangle. Push the corner again.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It won't move. A triangle can only change shape if one of its bars gets longer or shorter.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And steel is very, very hard to stretch or squash.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So each bar in a triangle is only pulled or pushed straight along its length.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's the job steel is best at. No bending, no twisting.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Chain lots of triangles together, and you get a truss.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It's light and full of holes, but incredibly stiff.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A heavy truck crosses, and the bridge sags just a few centimeters.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The same weight on a flat beam the same size would need much more steel.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why bridge builders in the 1800s fell in love with the truss.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Even your bike frame is basically two triangles.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know. What should we explain next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
